.TH CBMAP_CREATE_CH 3 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_create_ch \- create a BST map ordered by a custom comparison procedure
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

typedef int (*ccol_comparison_proc_t)(const void *first,
                                      const void *second);

cbmap cbmap_create_ch(ccol_data_type key_type,
                      ccol_comparison_proc_t custom_comparison_proc,
                      char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a self-balancing BST map whose keys are ordered by
custom_comparison_proc. It is a thin inline wrapper over
cbmap_create_full(3) that passes NULL for the memory management
procedures, so the map uses the default allocator. The type-inferred
equivalents are cbmap_construct_cc(3) and cbmap_init_cc(3).

The procedure only decides the order of the keys. key_type sets the width
that the map expects of every key_pair of a fixed-width key type, and it
also sets the alignment of every stored key.

cbmap_construct_cc(3) gives the full contract of the procedure. In short,
it returns a negative value, zero or a positive value, it defines key
equality as well as key order, and the order it gives must be consistent
and transitive for the whole life of the map.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
key_type                Type of the keys that the map stores
custom_comparison_proc  Comparison procedure, or NULL for the default
                        order for key_type
err                     Optional pointer that receives an error string on
                        failure, or NULL
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new map on success. On failure it returns NULL and, when err is not
NULL, sets *err to an error string.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The procedure gets two bare pointers to key bytes, and a key that comes in
through the raw layer can sit at any address. Read a key wider than one
byte with memcpy, not through a pointer cast to the key type. The
procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map.

This function reports a failure to the caller, while the
cbmap_construct_cc(3) and cbmap_init_cc(3) macros call _ccol_fatal_err()
on a failure.

Destroy the map with cbmap_destroy(3).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct { int major; int minor; } version;

int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b) {
    version x, y;
    memcpy(&x, a, sizeof(x));
    memcpy(&y, b, sizeof(y));
    if (x.major != y.major) {
        return (x.major > y.major) - (x.major < y.major);
    }
    return (x.minor > y.minor) - (x.minor < y.minor);
}

char *err = NULL;
cbmap releases = cbmap_create_ch(ccol_other_types, version_cmp, &err);
if (!releases) {
    fprintf(stderr, "%s\en", err);
    return 1;
}
/* ... cbmap_insert_elem(releases, ...) ... */
cbmap_destroy(releases);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_create (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3),
.BR cbmap_construct_cc (3),
.BR cbmap_init_cc (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_determine_ccol_data_type (3)
